{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00335 - MONDO_5068",
  "verbalization": "Atenolol, a beta-blocker, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Myocardial Infarction (MI) through a chain of indications that ultimately link it to the disease. The evidence suggests that Atenolol is associated with Coronary Thrombosis, which is a known risk factor for MI. Coronary Thrombosis is further linked to Alteplase and Phenprocoumon, both of which are indicated for MI. While Alteplase is a thrombolytic agent used to dissolve blood clots, Phenprocoumon is an anticoagulant that prevents clot formation. The indirect relationship between Atenolol and MI is mediated through these pharmacological interactions, with Atenolol potentially contributing to the development of thrombosis, which in turn increases the risk of MI. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Atenolol and MI through its role in promoting thrombosis, though the exact causal pathway remains partially inferred.",
